WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Recurrent neural networks are a workhorse for
sequence data,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:08.545
yet they famously struggle when the data carries
long-term dependencies.

00:00:08.625 --> 00:00:13.314
This paper asks why, from the rigorous viewpoint of
approximation theory.

00:00:13.394 --> 00:00:16.451
The authors prove an inverse, or Bernstein-type,

00:00:16.531 --> 00:00:22.004
theorem for nonlinear RNNs: any sequence
relationship that can be stably approximated

00:00:22.084 --> 00:00:26.121
by an RNN must have a memory that decays
exponentially in time.

00:00:26.201 --> 00:00:30.564
In other words, the difficulty is not just an
optimization artifact,

00:00:30.644 --> 00:00:34.419
it is a fundamental limitation of the RNN
hypothesis space,

00:00:34.499 --> 00:00:38.992
extending the known curse of memory from linear to
nonlinear networks.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.691
Building on this analysis,

00:00:40.771 --> 00:00:46.506
they propose a principled stable reparameterization
that lets RNNs escape the limitation,

00:00:46.586 --> 00:00:50.100
and they confirm everything with numerical experiments.
